{#
  module/SionModel/view/sion-model/sion-model/changes-table.phtml, ported.

  Expects: changes (list), max_rows (int|null), show_entity (bool).

  Kept a separate template because the original is a separate partial: SionModel renders
  `changes-table` from an entity's own change panel as well as from /sm/view-changes, so
  the next port to need it should include this rather than copy it.

  Five things here are the original's behaviour rather than choices, and every one of them
  would look like a bug to someone tidying up:

  1. **A fresh <thead> every time the date changes.** The original emits a new header row
     whenever the short date differs from the previous row's, so one table carries many
     <thead> elements. Invalid-ish, universally rendered, and what the page has always
     looked like.
  2. **`<span >`, with a stray quote, when a row has no ipAddress.** The original opens
     `<span ` and writes the tooltip attributes conditionally, but the closing `">` sits
     *outside* the condition. Reproduced, because the alternative is a port whose output
     differs.
  3. **updatedBy prints only when it is an array**, and only its `username`. A change made
     by a user id the users table no longer holds arrives as `['userId' => n]` — an array
     with no `username` — and the original prints nothing for it (an undefined-index
     notice, swallowed). `?? ''` is that same nothing, without the notice; Twig's
     strict_variables would otherwise make it an exception on a live page.
  4. **The row limit is applied here, in the view.** It is now also applied by the query
     (SionTable::getChanges), but keeping it here is what makes max_rows meaningful when a
     caller passes more rows than it wants shown.
  5. **oldValue/newValue truncate to 150 characters**, ellipsis included, via
     SionModel\Text\Text::truncate — the same static call the original makes.

  Escaping: Twig escapes every interpolation, which is what escapeHtml/escapeHtmlAttr did
  by hand. `entity()` returns markup and is declared is_safe, so it is the one value that
  is not escaped — exactly as the original's bare `echo` of formatEntity.
#}
{% import 'schoenstatt/_entity-format.html.twig' as fmt %}
<div class="responsive-table">
<table class="table">
{% set standing_date = null %}{% for row in changes %}{% if max_rows is null or loop.index <= max_rows %}{% set row_date = short_date(row.updatedOn) %}{% if row_date != standing_date %}{% set standing_date = row_date %}	<thead>
	<tr>
		<th>{{ row_date }}</th>{% if show_entity %}			<th>{{ translate('Entity') }}</th>{% endif %}		<th>{{ translate('Field') }}</th>
		<th>{{ translate('Old Value') }}</th>
		<th>{{ translate('New Value') }}</th>
	</tr>
	</thead>
	{% endif %}	<tr>
    	<td><span{% if row.ipAddress is defined and row.ipAddress %} data-toggle="tooltip" data-placement="bottom" data-container="body"
    	title="{{ row.ipAddress }}{% endif %}">{{ row.updatedBy is iterable ? (row.updatedBy.username ?? '') : '' }}</span>
    	</td>{% if show_entity %}		<td>{% if row.object is defined and row.object is not null %}{{ fmt.entity(row.entityType, row.object, {'displayEditPencil': (row.object.isDeleted ?? null) is null, 'failSilently': true}) }}{% endif %}</td>{% endif %}		<td>{{ row.field }}</td>
		<td>{{ truncate(row.oldValue, 150) }}</td>
		<td>{{ truncate(row.newValue, 150) }}</td>
	</tr>
{% endif %}{% endfor %}</table>
</div>
